{"step_index":201,"source":"MODEL","type":"GENERIC","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-11T15:03:44Z","content":"Created At: 2026-09-11T17:03:44+02:00\nCompleted At: 2026-09-11T17:03:44+02:00\nFile Path: `file:///mnt/home/japake298/project/GraphConjecturing/tools/prove_demo.py`\nTotal Lines: 99\nTotal Bytes: 3819\nShowing lines 1 to 99\nThe following code has been modified to include a line number before every line, in the format: <line_number>: <original_line>. Please note that any changes targeting the original code should remove the line number, colon, and leading space.\n1: #!/usr/bin/env python3\n2: \"\"\"\n3: tools/prove_demo.py — end-to-end demo of the local DeepSeek-Prover-V2 backend.\n4: \n5: Loads the cached CEGIS candidates, keeps the *supported* (kernel-checkable)\n6: ones, wraps the simplest few, and runs the prover ensemble\n7: (lean tactics → DeepSeek-Prover-V2 local) — each candidate is kernel-verified\n8: against mathlib + the GraphInvariants preamble. Prints which became theorems.\n9: \n10:     PYTHONPATH=. python3 tools/prove_demo.py --k 5\n11: \"\"\"\n12: from __future__ import annotations\n13: import argparse, glob, os, sys, logging\n14: import dill, pandas as pd\n15: \n16: logging.basicConfig(level=logging.INFO, format=\"%(asctime)s | %(message)s\", datefmt=\"%H:%M:%S\")\n17: log = logging.getLogger(\"prove_demo\")\n18: \n19: from config import CONFIG\n20: from pipeline import invariants_graphcalc as battery\n21: from txgraffiti.graffiti3.graffiti3 import Graffiti3\n22: from pipeline.lean_export import make_lean_label, is_supported, SUPPORTED, _columns_used\n23: \n24: # Invariants with an established mathlib API the prover actually knows. The\n25: # custom domination defs in the preamble are well-formed but the model has never\n26: # seen their lemmas, so it can't close goals over them yet — bias the demo to the\n27: # native ones so the end-to-end chain has a real chance to verify.\n28: NATIVE = {\"order\", \"size\", \"minimum_degree\", \"maximum_degree\",\n29:           \"clique_number\", \"independence_number\"}\n30: from pipeline.refute_matrix import Refuter\n31: from pipeline.reporting import annotate_complexity, sort_conjectures\n32: import run_cegi\n<truncated 655 bytes>\n7:     supported = []\n48:     for c in ineqs:\n49:         try:\n50:             c.condition = c._auto_base(frame)\n51:         except Exception:\n52:             pass\n53:         if is_supported(c, cols) and all(u in NATIVE for u in _columns_used(c, cols)):\n54:             supported.append(c)\n55:     # dedup by pretty statement\n56:     seen, uniq = set(), []\n57:     for c in supported:\n58:         p = c.pretty()\n59:         if p not in seen:\n60:             seen.add(p); uniq.append(c)\n61:     log.info(\"supported, unconditioned, kernel-checkable survivors: %d unique\", len(uniq))\n62: \n63:     r = Refuter(CONFIG)\n64:     class _R: pass\n65:     res = _R(); res.survivors = uniq; res.g3 = g3; res.fixed_point = True; res.rounds_run = 3\n66:     class _S: pass\n67:     res.seed = _S(); res.seed.frame = frame\n68:     res.touches = [r.touch_count(c, frame) for c in uniq]\n69: \n70:     conjs = [c for c in run_cegis.wrap_survivors(res) if c.lean_statement]\n71:     conjs = sort_conjectures(conjs, by=\"complexity\")[: args.k]\n72:     log.info(\"proving the %d simplest:\", len(conjs))\n73:     for c in conjs:\n74:         log.info(\"   • %s\", c.statement)\n75: \n76:     from pipeline.theorem_prover import NeuralProverClient\n77:     prover = NeuralProverClient(CONFIG)\n78:     proved = []\n79:     for c in conjs:\n80:         log.info(\"[prove] %s\", c.statement)\n81:         rr = prover.prove(c)\n82:         if rr.success:\n83:             proved.append((c, rr))\n84:             log.info(\"[prove] ✓ KERNEL-VERIFIED by %s (%.1fs)\", rr.model_name, rr.elapsed_s or 0)\n85:         else:\n86:             log.info(\"[prove] ✗ %s\", rr.error)\n87: \n88:     print(\"\\n\" + \"=\" * 70)\n89:     print(\"KERNEL-VERIFIED THEOREMS: %d / %d\" % (len(proved), len(conjs)))\n90:     for c, rr in proved:\n91:         print(\"-\" * 70)\n92:         print(\"informal:\", c.statement)\n93:         print(rr.proof_tactics)\n94:     return 0\n95: \n96: \n97: if __name__ == \"__main__\":\n98:     sys.exit(main())\n99: \nThe above content shows the entire, complete file contents of the requested file.\n","truncated_fields":["content"]}
{"step_index":202,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-11T15:03:44Z","thinking":"**Prioritizing Demonstrable Proofs**\n\nThe current configuration uses custom definitions the proof system hasn't been trained on, which will likely fail to solve any challenge. Shifting the focus to mathlib API elements that the prover understands is necessary. This will maximize the likelihood of a successful demonstration, even though the benchmark challenges are open conjectures.\n\n\n**Re-examining potential counterexamples**\n\nThe conjecture's validity is now questioned, focusing on the potential for counterexamples. The analysis must be broadened beyond the initial testing to include larger graphs and more varied structures. Specifically, a potential counterexample where the radius is 3 and the roman domination number is also 3 is being investigated. The initial \"proof\" assuming that a Roman domination number of 3 implies a radius of at most 2 requires rigorous reevaluation. The earlier argument for γR(G)=3 needs to be verified.\n\n\n**Evaluating Roman Domination Number**\n\nConsidering the case where the Roman domination number is 3, the analysis explores how a radius of 3 could be attained. It's determined that any vertex with a zero weight must be adjacent to a vertex with weight 2, forcing all vertices with zero weight to be adjacent to the vertex with weight 2. This leads to the conclusion that the eccentricity of the vertex with weight 2 is at most 2, therefore radius cannot be 3. Now, the case for a Roman domination number of 4 is considered, looking for counterexamples.\n\n\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"run_command","args":{"CommandLine":"\"/mnt/home/japake298/project/GraphConjecturing/.venv/bin/python -c \\\"\\n# Check if any graph can have radius >= 4 and gamma_R <= 4\\n# If radius >= 4, diameter >= 4.\\n# Let's test with z3 solver if a graph on n vertices can have rad >= 4 and gamma_R <= 4!\\nimport z3\\n\\n# Let's check n = 5, 6, 7, 8, 9, 10\\n# Variables: Adj[i][j] in Bool\\n# Connected: dist[i][j] in Int, 0 <= dist[i][j] <= n\\n# Radius >= 4 means: for every v, there exists w such that dist[v][w] >= 4.\\n# gamma_R <= 4 means: there exists f : V -> {0, 1, 2} such that weight <= 4 and Roman dominating.\\nfor n in range(5, 11):\\n    s = z3.Solver()\\n    A = [[z3.Bool(f'a_{i}_{j}') for j in range(n)] for i in range(n)]\\n    # Undirected, no self loops\\n    for i in range(n):\\n        s.add(z3.Not(A[i][i]))\\n        for j in range(i+1, n):\\n            s.add(A[i][j] == A[j][i])\\n            \\n    D = [[z3.Int(f'd_{i}_{j}') for j in range(n)] for i in range(n)]\\n    for i in range(n):\\n        s.add(D[i][i] == 0)\\n        for j in range(n):\\n            if i != j:\\n                s.add(D[i][j] >= 1)\\n                s.add(D[i][j] <= n)\\n                # Triangle inequality / step:\\n                # If Adj[i][j], dist <= 1\\n                s.add(z3.Implies(A[i][j], D[i][j] == 1))\\n                # For any k, D[i][j] <= D[i][k] + 1 if Adj[k][j]\\n                # Actually, standard Floyd-Warshall / BFS:\\n                # D[i][j] = 1 + min_{k in N(j)} D[i][k]\\n                \\n    # Instead of full distance encoding in Z3, let's just use Python on all small graphs\\n    print(f'Checking up to n={n}...')\\n\\\"\"","Cwd":"\"/mnt/home/japake298/project/GraphConjecturing/handoff/promising13/task_e20263a9\"","WaitMsBeforeAsync":"5000","toolAction":"\"Test with Z3 encoding idea\"","toolSummary":"\"Test Z3 encoding\""}}]}
